4. sharp and the @img/* platform binaries -- LGPL-3.0-or-later
================================================================================

This is the section that would make "every dependency is permissively licensed"
a false statement, so it is written out in full.

next 15.5.10 declares `sharp` as an OPTIONAL dependency, for image
optimisation. sharp 0.34.5 in turn declares 24 `@img/*` platform packages as
its own optional dependencies. In apps/console/package-lock.json every one of
them carries `"optional": true` and is constrained by `os` and `cpu`, so any
given machine installs only the handful that match its platform, never all 24.
None of them is needed: the console builds with image optimisation disabled,
and the artifact the production host runs has them deleted (see below).

Fourteen of those entries carry LGPL-3.0-or-later:

  @img/sharp-libvips-darwin-arm64     1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-darwin-x64       1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linux-arm        1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linux-arm64      1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linux-ppc64      1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linux-riscv64    1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linux-s390x      1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linux-x64        1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linuxmusl-arm64  1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-libvips-linuxmusl-x64    1.2.4   LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-win32-arm64              0.34.5  Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-win32-ia32               0.34.5  Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-win32-x64                0.34.5  Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-3.0-or-later
  @img/sharp-wasm32                   0.34.5  Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-3.0-or-later AND MIT

  All of the above are published by Lovell Fuller <npm@lovell.info>; sharp's
  own notice reads "Copyright 2013 Lovell Fuller and others."

  sharp:         https://github.com/lovell/sharp          (Apache-2.0)
  sharp-libvips: https://github.com/lovell/sharp-libvips
  libvips:       https://github.com/libvips/libvips
  LGPL-3.0 text: https://www.gnu.org/licenses/lgpl-3.0.html

The `@img/sharp-libvips-*` packages are prebuilt bundles of libvips and its
dependency stack, and each ships a README.md carrying a per-library license
table for what is inside it. The table in @img/sharp-libvips-linux-x64 1.2.4,
read while writing this file, lists 29 upstream projects: LGPLv3 for
libvips, glib, librsvg, pango, libheif, libexif, fribidi and proxy-libintl;
MPL-2.0 for cairo; and permissive terms (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0, zlib, libpng and
similar) for the remaining twenty, one of which -- aom -- also carries the
Alliance for Open Media Patent License 1.0. Those tables, not this file, are
the authoritative statement of the contents of those bundles.

What this repository does with them:

  - It does not modify sharp, libvips, or any `@img/*` package. They are
    consumed unmodified as published npm packages.

  - No source file in this repository imports sharp. apps/console/next.config.ts
    sets `images: { unoptimized: true }`, so nothing loads it at runtime.
    Next.js traces it into the standalone output anyway, which is why the
    removal below exists.

Which artifact contains them:

Neither. As of the commits that added the checks described below, no image
built from this repository can contain them -- and that is enforced by the
build, not by a instruction someone has to remember.

  - The recipe under "Producing the console artifact" in deploy/RUNBOOK.md --
    the one that produces deploy/console-dist.tgz, which is what
    deploy/Dockerfile.console.prebuilt extracts and what the production host
    runs -- executes `rm -rf .next/standalone/node_modules/@img` before packing.

  - deploy/Dockerfile.console builds from source. It now performs the same
    removal after `npm ci`, rather than relying on the runbook.

  - Both deploy/Dockerfile.console and deploy/Dockerfile.console.prebuilt end
    with an assertion over the assembled image: if any `@img` directory or any
    `libvips*.so*` survives, the build fails. It matches the shared object
    rather than today's package names, so a rename upstream does not defeat it.

The assertion was verified in both directions with real builds, and with the
assertion deleted, so that its absence is known to be what lets a contaminated
tree through: a tarball carrying `@img` fails the build and the failure names
both `libvips-cpp.so.8.17.3` files; the same tarball with the assertion removed
builds green at 241 MB with the payload inside; a clean tarball builds at
207 MB with no `@img` and no `libvips*.so*` anywhere in the image.

This is why `--omit=optional` was not used instead: eight of the 38 optional
entries are `@next/swc-*`, next's native compiler, declared in the same
`optionalDependencies` block as sharp, with no `@next/swc-wasm-nodejs` in the
lockfile to fall back to. Dropping them would remove the compiler `next build`
needs. Removing `@img` is sufficient because all fourteen LGPL-bearing entries
live under `@img/`.

Should an image ever be conveyed that does contain these packages -- built by
bypassing the assertion, or from a commit predating it -- the LGPL-3.0
obligations apply to that conveyance: the license text must travel with it, the
use of the library must be stated, and the recipient must be able to obtain the
library's source and to relink a modified version. The upstream sources are at
the URLs above, published free of charge and without an account. This paragraph
records the obligation. It does not assert that such an image has been built or
distributed.


